WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.968
Welcome to Module 5, Checking Data
Quality. In this module, we

00:00:03.968 --> 00:00:04.800
will use GGIR

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.096
summaries, plots, and protocol rules to
decide whether

00:00:08.096 --> 00:00:09.500
processed accelerometer

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.868
files are trustworthy enough to clean and
analyze. Welcome to

00:00:14.868 --> 00:00:15.660
Module 5,

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.727
Checking Data Quality. By the end of this
lesson, you should

00:00:18.727 --> 00:00:19.800
be able to locate the

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.811
main GGIR quality-control outputs,
interpret clipping_score,

00:00:23.811 --> 00:00:24.480
calib_err,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.740
and complete_24hcycle; distinguish
inspection outputs from

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.366
communication reports, and document each
decision before final

00:00:32.366 --> 00:00:33.360
cleaning. Because

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.064
GGIR file names and report formats can
change across versions,

00:00:37.064 --> 00:00:37.900
always confirm

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.860
the outputs produced by your own run.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:46.021
Processing is not the same as quality
control. Start with the

00:00:46.021 --> 00:00:47.180
GGIR output, then

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:50.850
combine three kinds of evidence. Numerical
QC summaries,

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.620
visual plots, and the study

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.524
protocol. Summaries flag calibration,
clipping, file health,

00:00:56.524 --> 00:00:57.760
and coverage. Plots

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.271
reveal patterns that one number may miss.
Protocol rules

00:01:01.271 --> 00:01:02.400
define what counts

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.760
as usable data. No single flag makes the
decision

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.680
automatically. Together, the

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.963
evidence supports a defensible keep,
review, or exclude

00:01:11.963 --> 00:01:13.520
outcome. Here is the

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.956
usual map of GGIR quality control outputs,
although exact

00:01:16.956 --> 00:01:17.920
folders and file

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.369
names depend on version and settings. In
current GGIR, the

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:22.320
Part 2 summaries

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.166
contain indicators such as clipping_score,
calib_err, and

00:01:26.166 --> 00:01:27.380
complete_24hcycle.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:31.172
cycle. The QC folder adds file health and
calibration details

00:01:31.172 --> 00:01:31.980
together with

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.392
per-recording plots. File summary reports
provide a higher

00:01:35.392 --> 00:01:35.980
resolution

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.491
visual review. If your local workflow
creates files called

00:01:39.491 --> 00:01:40.580
time report, treat

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.828
that as a project-specific or older label
and verify which

00:01:44.828 --> 00:01:46.220
output produced it.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:51.074
This GGIR run contains config.csv, a
results folder, and a

00:01:51.074 --> 00:01:52.580
meta folder. Start

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:55.706
quality control in results. The results
folder contains

00:01:55.706 --> 00:01:56.900
participant-level and

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.610
day-level summaries, as well as QC
outputs, while meta stores

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:01.340
intermediate

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.333
objects used by GGIR. The exact structure
can vary by version

00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:06.380
and settings, so

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.145
first, confirm which outputs were
generated. We will now open

00:02:10.145 --> 00:02:10.700
the files

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:15.448
needed for the quality review. This
distinction is version

00:02:15.448 --> 00:02:16.840
sensitive. Legacy

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:20.760
GGIR visual reports, often named old
report, were mainly

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.020
participant facing

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:25.395
and should not be the primary basis for
quality control.

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:26.600
Current report files

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.806
generated by the visual report option are
designed to support

00:02:30.806 --> 00:02:31.840
QC. Use them to

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.005
inspect the time series, then confirm
concerns in the

00:02:35.005 --> 00:02:35.960
quantitative CSV

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:40.254
summaries and Part 2 quality plots.
Visuals help identify a

00:02:40.254 --> 00:02:41.200
question. The

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.664
summaries and pre-specified protocol
determine what the

00:02:45.664 --> 00:02:46.800
finding means.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.756
Open results, then QC, then
data_quality_report.csv, review

00:02:52.756 --> 00:02:53.160
file

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.418
corruption, short recordings, calibration
start and end

00:02:56.418 --> 00:02:57.840
values, QC messages, and

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.210
any file health fields that apply to your
device. Three

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:03.640
indicators deserve

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:07.192
careful interpretation. clipping_score
summarizes the fraction

00:03:07.192 --> 00:03:07.880
of recording

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.196
windows affected by values near the
sensor's dynamic range.

00:03:11.196 --> 00:03:12.320
Ideally, it is zero.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:16.289
calib_err is the residual static estimate
after

00:03:16.289 --> 00:03:17.640
autocalibration.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:22.339
Interpret its value and unit using the
installed version's

00:03:22.339 --> 00:03:23.960
output documentation

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:28.102
and the study-approved rule.
complete_24hcycle is a

00:03:28.102 --> 00:03:29.320
recording-level

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.240
coverage score, not a Boolean
day-completeness flag. Treat all

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.131
three as screening evidence and
cross-check the plots. In

00:03:37.131 --> 00:03:38.360
part2_summary.csv,

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.960
locate clipping_score, calib_err, and
complete_24hcycle.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.410
cycle. Clipping should normally be zero.
Compare calibration

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:47.560
error with the study

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:51.254
approved threshold and interpret
completeness as a

00:03:51.254 --> 00:03:52.880
recording-level score.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.978
Visual inspection can reveal problems that
summary values

00:03:56.978 --> 00:03:58.200
hide. A long flat

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.480
segment may suggest non-wear, a stationary
device, or a sensor

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.846
malfunction. An abrupt shift may reflect
handling or a

00:04:04.846 --> 00:04:06.280
placement change. Also,

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.100
check recording length, long gaps, and
whether a full day and

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:09.840
night pattern is

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.662
present. These features are review flags,
not automatic

00:04:13.662 --> 00:04:15.260
exclusions. Compare the

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:19.209
plot with the QC summary, file metadata,
wear logs, and study

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:20.180
schedule before

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:25.455
deciding what happened. Open the
plots_part2 folder and review

00:04:25.455 --> 00:04:25.880
every

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:30.129
participant PNG. Yellow typically marks
standard non-wear,

00:04:30.129 --> 00:04:31.740
brown marks additional

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:35.387
non-wear filtering, and purple marks
clipping. Flag unusual

00:04:35.387 --> 00:04:36.500
gaps, long invalid

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.580
blocks, or patterns that do not match the
wear log.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.768
Next, open the file summary report for the
same recording.

00:04:44.768 --> 00:04:45.540
This higher

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.631
resolution view helps you compare daily
acceleration, wear

00:04:49.631 --> 00:04:50.900
patterns, sleep or

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:54.798
behavior classifications, and missing
segments. In newer GGIR

00:04:54.798 --> 00:04:55.820
versions, the QC

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:59.528
oriented report is generally named report.
Older projects may

00:04:59.528 --> 00:05:00.500
use time report.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:04.938
Review it together with the summary CSV
and the Part 2 plot.

00:05:04.938 --> 00:05:05.900
Do not base a

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:11.040
decision on a single visualization. A
reproducible QC process

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.220
needs an audit

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:15.754
trail. Record the participant ID, exact
file name, review

00:05:15.754 --> 00:05:17.180
date, reviewer, concern

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.452
category, action taken, and whether a
second opinion is

00:05:20.452 --> 00:05:21.820
required. Also note the

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.690
observed value or visual evidence and the
rule or threshold

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:26.740
applied. One row

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:30.118
per reviewed file is a useful minimum. If
a file has several

00:05:30.118 --> 00:05:31.300
independent concerns,

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:34.811
record them clearly. Complete the log
during review so another

00:05:34.811 --> 00:05:35.660
team member can

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:40.480
reconstruct the decision without guessing.
This slide combines

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:41.180
day-level

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:44.906
and participant-level rules. In this
course example, a day is

00:05:44.906 --> 00:05:45.700
valid when it

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:49.151
contains at least 10 hours of awake wear.
A participant is

00:05:49.151 --> 00:05:50.460
eligible when at least

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.848
four such valid days are available,
including at least one

00:05:53.848 --> 00:05:54.900
weekend day. These

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.933
are study-specific example criteria, not
universal GGIR

00:05:58.933 --> 00:06:00.620
defaults. Before coding

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.932
them in Stata, confirm how awake wear and
weekend days are

00:06:03.932 --> 00:06:04.960
represented in the

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:09.760
selected output and document any
exceptions. Use

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.760
part2_daysummary.csv

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.070
for the day-level review. Filter one
participant at a time.

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:16.080
Check the calendar

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.947
date and weekday, then inspect valid hours
for every

00:06:18.947 --> 00:06:20.160
measurement day. Count

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.590
the days meeting the 10-hour rule and
confirm that at least

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:24.520
one is a weekend

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:29.240
day. The public QC manifest provides
anonymous review codes

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:30.200
and a review

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:34.203
status. Each item begins as pending
review. After checking the

00:06:34.203 --> 00:06:34.720
matching

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.672
summary, plot, report, and valid-day
evidence, update the

00:06:37.672 --> 00:06:38.760
study record to keep,

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.237
review, or exclude. The separate private
manifest links each

00:06:42.237 --> 00:06:43.280
review code to its

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.905
actual file. Record the reviewer, date,
concern, and rationale

00:06:46.905 --> 00:06:47.840
according to the

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:52.134
study's documentation procedure. The final
QC decision

00:06:52.134 --> 00:06:54.520
integrates the log, summaries,

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:57.899
plots, and pre-specified study rules. Keep
a record when the

00:06:57.899 --> 00:06:58.800
criteria are met

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.671
and no important concern remains
unresolved. Send borderline

00:07:02.671 --> 00:07:02.800
or

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.168
conflicting evidence for review, ideally
to a second reviewer.

00:07:06.168 --> 00:07:07.200
Exclude only when a

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:10.454
documented rule is clearly failed and
specify whether that

00:07:10.454 --> 00:07:11.520
decision applies to

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:14.910
a day, a file, or the entire participant.
A warning alone does

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:15.840
not automatically

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.291
require exclusion. Record the evidence and
rationale, then

00:07:19.291 --> 00:07:20.600
carry the final status

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:24.320
into cleaning and standardization.
